            <ArticleTitle>Neuropsychiatric scurvy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>12-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.psym.2014.05.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0033-3182(14)00085-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Scurvy is a disease with well-known peripheral symptoms, such as bleeding and pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The clinical and historical evidence for a distinct form of scurvy affecting the central nervous system, called neuropsychiatric scurvy, is reviewed. Pathophysiologic factors are described, as well as its diagnosis and management.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
